-# What key erasure guarantees on each key store
 
-A data protection officer asks one question about a right-to-erasure request: once the erasure call has returned, can anything still decrypt the person's data? The answer is not the same on every key store. Chronicle deletes the key the same way everywhere, but what "deleted" means after that depends on the store holding the key, and on how you deployed it.
+<a id="what-key-erasure-guarantees-on-each-key-store"></a>
 
-This page states the guarantee for each store, what Chronicle cannot promise, and what you configure to keep the guarantee. It does not claim that erasure is instant or complete on every backend. For the mechanism itself, read [The encryption key lifecycle](key-lifecycle.mdx); to choose and configure a store, read [Compliance Storage](../hosting/configuration/compliance-storage.md).
+A successful Cratis Chronicle erasure call confirms that its key-store operations completed; it does not prove that no copy of the key can still decrypt the subject's data. MongoDB deletes documents, HashiCorp Vault deletes secret metadata and versions, and Azure Key Vault requires a purge after soft deletion. Replication, recovery rights and backups affect what remains readable.
+
+Use this page to define what your erasure procedure can promise on those three stores. For the fence mechanism, read [The encryption key lifecycle](key-lifecycle.mdx); for connection settings and permissions, use [Compliance Storage](../hosting/configuration/compliance-storage.md).
 
 ## What Chronicle does the same everywhere
 
-An erasure records the [erasure fence](key-lifecycle.mdx#the-erasure-fence) first and then deletes every revision of the subject's key. The key store is wrapped in an in-process key cache; the cache is invalidated before and after the delete, and the erasure evicts the key from every silo's cache. After the erasure returns, Chronicle refuses to provision or accept a key for that subject until you authorize a new one. Reads are not checked against the fence: if the store still returns key material for the subject (a replica that has not caught up, a recovered or restored secret), Chronicle decrypts with it. The erasure is only as complete as the store's own delete, which is what the sections below describe.
+Chronicle attempts to record the [erasure fence](key-lifecycle.mdx#the-erasure-fence) in every event store in the namespace before deleting key revisions. It also removes unique-index claims that reserve values for the subject and evicts the key from every silo's cache. A silo is a Chronicle kernel node. The cache wrapper invalidates local entries before and after deletion.
+
+A readable fence blocks automatic provisioning until a new key is authorized and continues to reject destroyed key material afterwards. It does not validate every read. Individual stores can still return stale, restored or recovered material; a composed store checks readable members' fences, but an unavailable member's fence can be missed. Successful deletion therefore depends on the backend, not just the fence.
 
-Those guarantees are about Chronicle. They do not reach:
+Stop the subject's appends and forwarding before erasure. Persistent backends check the fence and write keys in separate operations, so an already-running write can persist after deletion. Repeat erasure if writes overlapped it.
 
-- Values that were never encrypted, or that were already delivered as plaintext. Key deletion cannot change what was read before it.
-- Backups, replicas and snapshots of the key store that you or your platform made. See [what the fence cannot protect against](key-lifecycle.mdx#not-stopped) and [backup and restore ordering](../hosting/configuration/compliance-storage.md#backup-and-restore-ordering).
