+A membership can end because one user leaves or because the group is disbanded. Declare both handlers on the membership collection:
 
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="projections/declarative/remove-with-join/user-groups-example" />
 
-In this example:
-
-- **`RemovedWith<UserLeftGroup>`**: Removes membership when a user explicitly leaves a group
-- **`RemovedWithJoin<GroupDisbanded>`**: Removes membership from all users when a group is disbanded
+The user-left handler supplies both `UserId` and `GroupId`, so it removes one membership. The group-disbanded handler supplies the group key, so it removes that group from every user's membership collection in this projection.
 
 ## Complex scenario: Project assignments
 
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="projections/declarative/remove-with-join/project-assignments-example" />
 
-This handles three removal scenarios:
+The same distinction applies to project assignments:
 
-1. **Individual unassignment**: `DeveloperUnassignedFromProject` removes one developer from one project
-2. **Project cancellation**: `ProjectCancelled` removes the project from all developers
-3. **Project completion**: `ProjectCompleted` removes the project from all developers
+- An unassignment removes one project from one developer's collection.
+- A cancellation removes that project from all developer collections in the projection.
+- A completion removes it from those collections in the same way.
 
 ## Read model examples
 
+Each parent keeps its own collection of memberships or assignments. The child identifier is the group or project ID shared with the removal event:
+
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="projections/declarative/remove-with-join/read-models" />
 
 ## Event definitions
 
+Individual removal events carry the parent and child IDs. Group-wide and project-wide removal events can use their event source ID as the child key:
+
 <ChronicleClientTabs snippet="projections/declarative/remove-with-join/events" />
 
 ## Best practices
 
 ### Use for cross-stream cleanup
 
-`RemovedWithJoin<>()` is ideal when:
-
-- Events from one stream should trigger cleanup in projections of other stream
-- You need to maintain referential integrity across stream boundaries
-- Deletion or deactivation events should cascade to related read models
+Use `RemovedWithJoin<>()` when an event about one entity should remove its representation from many parent collections. This updates derived read models; it does not delete the source events or enforce a write-time relationship constraint.
 
 ### Combine with RemovedWith
 
-Often you'll use both removal methods together:
-
-- `RemovedWith<>()` for explicit removals within the same stream
-- `RemovedWithJoin<>()` for cascade removals from related streams
+Choose by the scope of the removal, not just by which stream emitted the event. Use `RemovedWith<>()` when you know the parent and child. Use `RemovedWithJoin<>()` when the child key should match across parents.
 
 ### Consider event ordering
 
-Be aware that `RemovedWithJoin<>()` events might be processed before all related `Join<>()` events have completed, so ensure your system handles partial data gracefully.
+Parent instances can be updated by different event-source partitions. Do not treat removal across their collections as a single atomic change. See [eventual consistency in projections](../eventual-consistency) when deciding what callers may read while processing catches up.
 
-The `RemovedWithJoin<>()` method provides powerful cross-stream cleanup capabilities, ensuring that your read models stay consistent when related data is removed from other parts of your system.
+If you are still choosing the child identifier or routing membership events to a parent, start with [child projections](./children).
